{
  "status": "complete",
  "summary": "Direct research on prompt-intake workflows is thinner than the market attention around prompt engineering suggests, but the available evidence is still useful. The strongest cross-source pattern is: define a precise elicitation objective, gather context/problem/examples before polishing wording, and use a hybrid workflow that produces an early draft and then refines it against concrete test cases. Recent clarifying-question research supports targeted, specific follow-ups rather than generic clarification, while requirements-engineering literature strongly supports separating discovery questions from confirmation questions and surfacing tacit assumptions through artifacts and observation. Evidence is weak on an exact optimal number of questions and on how professional prompt consultants work in the wild; most prompt-specific workflow guidance is vendor-authored, so vendor bias is relevant.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "High-value intake questions are the ones that either fill a missing prompt section or change the elicitation objective; a practical intake frame is Identity, Instructions, Examples, and Context.",
      "source": "https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-engineering | https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "OpenAI says developer prompts usually contain Identity, Instructions, Examples, and Context. IREB says the elicitation objective is the \"central\" concept and should guide sources, technique choice, and success measurement."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The first intake pass should map task context before wording details: stakeholders, documents, processes, events, history, constraints, and prior failed attempts.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "IREB recommends understanding project history first, including why the project started, prior failed attempts, and constraints; it also recommends understanding system context by identifying people, systems, documents, processes, and events."
    },
    {
      "claim": "When a user proposes a prompt or feature, translate it back into the underlying problem, goal, beneficiary, and failure consequence before you build.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "IREB says thinking in terms of problems and goals helps you dig deeper before \"jumping to\" an obvious solution, and warns to verify that a suggested solution actually solves the problem and realizes the goal."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Representative examples and ideal outputs are among the highest-leverage prompt inputs; if the user can provide only a little context, ask for 3-5 examples first.",
      "source": "https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompt-improver | https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/multishot-prompting | https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/prompt-optimizer",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Anthropic's prompt improver asks for example inputs and ideal outputs, and its multishot guide recommends 3-5 diverse, relevant examples. OpenAI's prompt optimizer asks for at least 3 data rows plus annotations or graders."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Specific clarifying questions outperform generic clarification requests, and they matter most when the user's initial request is short or ambiguous.",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.01934",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Rahmani et al. (2024) report that \"specific questions are more effective than generic ones\" and that shorter, more ambiguous queries benefit significantly from clarification."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Follow-up questioning works better when guided by a taxonomy of common interviewer mistakes than when generated ad hoc.",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.02858",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Shen, Singhal, and Breaux (accepted at RE 2025) found LLM-generated follow-up questions were no worse than human-authored ones on clarity, relevance, and informativeness, and outperformed human-authored questions when guided by interviewer mistake types."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Use a two-pass questioning pattern: open-ended questions to discover unknowns, then closed-ended questions to verify hypotheses and lock decisions.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "IREB says requirements elicitation predominantly needs open-ended questions because it seeks qualitative data, while closed-ended questions are better for verifying already-elicited requirements or confirming/rejecting hypotheses."
    },
    {
      "claim": "To surface tacit constraints, ask for artifacts, prior outputs, screenshots, transcripts, or observed workflows instead of relying only on verbal self-report.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "IREB says observation helps gather information that cannot be expressed verbally, helps elicit implicit knowledge, and notes that stakeholders often tell only part of the whole story."
    },
    {
      "claim": "A direct, high-value kickoff question is: 'What assumptions are baked into this goal statement?' because goals routinely hide unstated constraints.",
      "source": "https://discovery.bloomworks.digital/sections/prepare/ | https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Bloomworks advises teams to document any assumptions baked into their goals. IREB's discussion of transformation effects says stakeholder statements lose and distort information between reality, interpretation, and expression."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Do not front-load a rigid checklist or taxonomy; collect the user's raw description first, then apply structure later.",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23684",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Obaidi et al. (accepted at RE 2025) found that delayed taxonomy introduction produced a greater number and diversity of elicited needs and concluded that a two-phase approach is beneficial."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The best-supported workflow is hybrid rather than purely interrogate-first or purely build-first: do minimal objective-setting, build a first draft early, then interrogate against that draft and its failures.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf | https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompt-generator | https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompt-improver | https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23684",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Inference from multiple sources: IREB says a mixture between breadth-first and depth-first elicitation is advisable; Anthropic's tool flow is first draft, then variables, then improvement; Obaidi et al. found a delayed-structure two-phase process beneficial."
    },
    {
      "claim": "There is no robust research-backed exact number of upfront questions; a better stop rule is to stop when the next answer will no longer change the prompt, examples, constraints, or eval rubric.",
      "source": "https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf | https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.00235 | https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.12451",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Inference from adjacent evidence: IREB says \"the longer the questionnaire, the fewer the responses,\" while conversational-search work warns that asking clarifying questions for every ambiguity can drain patience and hamper user experience."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Define the evaluator early by asking who will decide the prompt succeeded and what a good vs. bad output looks like; prompts refine faster when judgment is externalized.",
      "source": "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/prompt-optimizer | https://www.gasq.org/files/content/gasq/downloads/certification/IREB/IREB%20AL/advanced_level_elicitation_handbook_en_v2.2.0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "IREB says the elicitation objective should make success measurable. OpenAI's optimizer explicitly improves prompts from grader results, human annotations, and detailed critiques, which operationalize what 'good' means."
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [],
  "gaps": [
    "I did not find controlled studies that directly compare full prompt-intake scripts or questionnaires against final prompt quality across multiple tasks.",
    "I did not find strong independent field studies on how professional prompt engineers or AI consultants structure client intake; most prompt-specific process guidance is vendor-authored.",
    "I did not find rigorous evidence for an exact optimal number of clarification questions before diminishing returns; the best available evidence is indirect and comes from requirements elicitation and conversational-search research.",
    "Evidence on build-first vs. interrogate-first is mostly cross-domain and inferential rather than prompt-engineering-specific."
  ],
  "escalations": []
}
